# Lesson 3, new exercise: the student holds the big hand. Count losers, draw trumps, and throw a heart on dummy's diamonds.
title: A big hand, a weak dummy
id: L03-16
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3
student: S
board: 70
dealer: E
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  876     65     KQ4    86542
  E  43      987    T632   JT93
  S  AKQJ92  A432   A5     A
  W  T5      KQJT   J987   KQ7

auction:
  E Pass
  S 2S*    = 22+ points, at least five spades: forcing
     key: 22 high-card points and six spades. Open with a strong two-bid.
     not 1S: Did you count your points? With 22 or more, partner might pass a one-bid when you have game in your own hand.
     not *: 22 high-card points and six spades: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = The usual answer to a strong two-bid
  E Pass
  S 4S*    = Game
     key: Partner may have almost nothing, but your hand is worth game on its own.
     not 3S: Did you count your tricks? Six spades and three aces: game is close even opposite nothing.
     not *: Your hand is worth game by itself. What is the spade game?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened a strong 2♠, and partner gave the usual 2NT answer. With game
  in your own hand, you bid 4♠. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 2
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 2 = Three small ones, but dummy can ruff one. Another may go away on dummy's third diamond.
  diamonds: 0 = The ace, king and queen.
  clubs: 0 = The ace.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Dummy has three diamonds to your two. Could the extra one help with your hearts?
play:
  1   W HK  N H5  E H7  S HA
      note: Three losers, all in hearts. That's all you can afford, and dummy's diamonds can get rid of one. First, draw trumps.
  2   S SA  W S5  N S6  E S3
      note: Draw trumps.
  3   S SK  W ST  N S7  E S4
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Trumps are drawn.
  4   S DA  W D7  N D4  E D2
      note: Now the diamonds. Play your ace first, the high card from the short side.
  5   S D5  W D8  N DK  E D3
      note: Then over to dummy's ♦K.
  6   N DQ  E D6  S H2*  W D9
      H2 key: Throw away a losing heart. Dummy's ♦Q is already winning.
      H2 not *: Did you count your losers? Your small hearts are losers: throw one away.
      note: You have no diamonds left. Throw away a losing heart.
  7   N C2  E C3  S CA  W C7
      note: Back to your hand with the ♣A.
  8   S H3  W HQ  N H6  E H8
      note: Now give up a heart. After this, dummy has no hearts left, and still has one trump.
  9   W HJ  N S8*  E H9  S H4
      S8 key: Dummy has no hearts left, so it ruffs.
      S8 not *: Did you notice that dummy has no hearts left? Its last trump can ruff.
      note: Dummy ruffs West's heart winner.
  10  N C4  E C9  S S2  W CQ
      note: Ruff a club to get back to your hand. The rest are yours.
  11  S SQ  W CK  N C5  E CT
      note: Your trumps are all winners.
  12  S SJ  W DJ  N C6  E CJ
      note: Another trump.
  13  S S9  W HT  N C8  E DT
      note: 12 tricks: 4♠ made with two overtricks.

play recap:
  The plan: count three heart losers, draw trumps, and throw one heart away
  on dummy's third diamond. Dummy's last trump then ruffed another.

  Dummy had only five points, but its ♦KQ, the extra diamond and one small
  trump turned two of your losers into winners.
